    "evidence_text": "Epigenetic regulation of miR-184 by MBD1 governs neural stem cell proliferation and differentiation.\n\nTherefore, MBD1, miR-184, and Numbl form a regulatory network that helps control the balance between proliferation and differentiation of aNSCs.\n\nmiR-302 Is Required for Timing of Neural Differentiation, Neural Tube Closure, and Embryonic Viability\n\nThe evolutionarily conserved miR-302 family of microRNAs is expressed during early mammalian embryonic development.\n\nThe neuroepithelium shows increased progenitor proliferation, decreased cell death, and precocious neuronal differentiation.\n\nmRNA profiling at multiple time points during neurulation uncovers a complex pattern of changing targets over time.\n\nOur results show that mir-302 helps regulate neurulation by suppressing neural progenitor expansion and precocious differentiation.\n\nFurthermore, these results uncover redundant roles for mir-290 and mir-302 early in development.\n\nProx1 Is Required for Granule Cell Maturation and Intermediate Progenitor Maintenance During Brain Neurogenesis\n\nThe homeobox gene Prox1 is expressed in the dentate gyrus during embryonic development and adult neurogenesis.\n\nFinally, we show that the ectopic expression of Prox1 induces premature differentiation of neural stem cells.\n\nDual Regulation of miRNA Biogenesis Generates Target Specificity in Neurotrophin-Induced Protein Synthesis\n\nControl of translation is a fundamental source of regulation in gene expression.\n\nWe report that BDNF rapidly elevates Dicer, increasing mature miRNA levels and inducing RNA processing bodies in neurons.\n\nBinding sites for Lin28-regulated miRNAs are necessary and sufficient to confer BDNF responsiveness to a transcript.\n\nAdult Neurogenesis Is Sustained by Symmetric Self-Renewal and Differentiation.\n\nSomatic stem cells have been identified in multiple adult tissues.\n\nWhether self-renewal occurs symmetrically or asymmetrically is key to understanding long-term stem cell maintenance and generation of progeny for cell replacement.\n\nThe mechanism of B1 cell retention into adulthood for lifelong neurogenesis is unknown.\n\nUsing multiple clonal labeling techniques, we show that the vast majority of B1 cells divide symmetrically.\n\nThis cellular mechanism decouples self-renewal from the generation of progeny.\n\nLimited rounds of symmetric self-renewal and consuming symmetric differentiation divisions can explain the levels of neurogenesis observed throughout life.\n\nEpigenetic regulation of NANOG by miR-302 cluster-MBD2 completes induced pluripotent stem cell reprogramming.\n\nPotential role of NF-kappaB in adult neural stem cells: the underrated steersman?\n\nNeural stem cells are precursors of neurons and glial cells.\n\nDuring brain development, these cells proliferate, migrate and differentiate into specific lineages.\n\nRecently neural stem cells within the adult central nervous system were identified.\n\nInformations are now emerging about regulation of stem cell proliferation, migration and differentiation by numerous soluble factors such as chemokines and cytokines.\n\nHowever, the signal transduction mechanisms downstream of these factors are less clear.\n\nNF-kappaB is an inducible transcription factor detected in neurons, glia and neural stem cells.\n\nRecent data suggest an important role of NF-kappaB on proliferation, migration and differentiation of neural stem cells.\n\nWithin the nucleus, several target genes could be activated.\n\nIn this review, we suggest a model explaining the multiple action of NF-kappaB on neural stem cells.\n\nFurthermore, we discuss the potential role of NF-kappaB within the so-called brain cancer stem cells.\n\nCD34+ hematopoietic stem-progenitor cell microRNA expression and function: a circuit diagram of differentiation control.\n\nmiRNAs play critical roles in many different cellular processes including metabolism, apoptosis, differentiation, and development.\n\nWe validated miRNA control of several of these target mRNAs by demonstrating that their translation in fact is decreased by miRNAs.\n\nQuiescent and active hippocampal neural stem cells with distinct morphologies respond selectively to physiological and pathological stimuli and aging.\n\nWe show that canonical Notch signaling through RBP-J is required for hippocampal neurogenesis.\n\nRadial and horizontal NSCs respond selectively to neurogenic stimuli.\n\nPhysical exercise activates the quiescent radial population whereas epileptic seizures induce expansion of the horizontal NSC pool.\n\nmiR-294/miR-302 promotes proliferation, suppresses G1-S restriction point, and inhibits ESC differentiation through separable mechanisms.\n\nUnder normal growth conditions, miR-294 promoted the rapid G1-S transition independent of the Rb family.\n\nFour of the six differentiation-inducing miRNAs induced an Rb-dependent G1 accumulation.\n\nHowever, all six still silenced self-renewal in the absence of the Rb proteins.",
